00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:55.500
I got to just, you know, beat up till they vanish off screen.

00:16:56.020 --> 00:17:01.260
Well, there's a couple set pieces that like there's the dudes on the motorcycles.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:06.340
And you have to kick them off their motorcycle before you can progress.

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:09.000
Like I think, I think they do like two at a time.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:11.640
And maybe this is just because it was taking me a long time.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.140
But I just noticed that these motorcycles kept coming.

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:17.120
And like that one would fall off.

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:19.160
I'd be fighting him on foot.

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:21.220
And then another one would come on the motorcycle.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:25.900
I feel like you had to kick both of them off their motorcycles or they would just keep getting replaced.

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:30.200
So that's a set piece.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:36.280
Sometimes they introduce these throwable or just weapons.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:42.980
But not like enough that they would affect anything beyond the scene that they're introduced in.

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:51.000
And sometimes you'll find like a fire extinguisher you could throw or a metal grate garbage can.

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:54.800
So I wouldn't say there's nothing revolutionary in there.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:56.380
It's stuff that we've seen.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:18:01.880
And just looking through the manual, it does kind of give you like a breakdown of the level to level.

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:06.260
And again, it kind of does emphasize some of the funnier things.

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:10.840
Like this is no place to hold a picnic unless you invite the skate lords.

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:12.660
Oh, they're the skate lords.

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:13.820
They're the skate lords.

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:17.880
And in level six, maybe you could help explain the joke here.

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:20.700
You're getting closer to Mr.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:21.860
Big's pig pen.

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:24.200
Watch out for the karate clan.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:26.980
They can make coleslaw out of anyone.

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:29.560
What's the coleslaw in reference to?

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.000
That's a complete non sequitur.

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:36.480
Okay, so there's no relationship between coleslaw and the karate clan.

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:43.500
Maybe in the deep lore, but from the description you've given me, it makes zero sense.

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:49.800
Yeah, you know, you really have to play renegade to understand all the references in target renegade.

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:52.460
You can't just jump into target renegade.

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:59.460
One thing I would like to say is beyond the enemy design and all this stuff that we've talked about,

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:04.340
in general, the vibe of the game is like super rad.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:07.580
Like this is one of the raddest games I've played.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:11.960
Yeah, this game is just basically following up on tons of 80s tropes.

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:16.820
Yeah, so you've got your status bar that tells you to go left or right.

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:25.320
Like that's saying like, yo kid, like go on and dust them or like watch your back and stuff like that.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:30.260
And the music is intense.

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:32.160
It's pretty intense music.

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:41.140
Like I think 80% of the soundtrack is midified guitar solos, which I really appreciate.

00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:43.160
Yeah, that opening is hot.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:44.380
It's very hot.

00:19:45.860 --> 00:19:50.780
Yeah, it's very radical and tubular.

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:56.700
Okay, and then the last thing, because beat-em-ups are inherently an arcade feeling thing.

00:19:56.700 --> 00:20:03.080
However, playing this on an NES, I felt more like I was playing, even though the game is relatively short,

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:09.600
I felt like I was playing a story mode version where like I'm moving between these scenes and these levels

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:11.680
and I'm getting closer to Mr.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:18.720
Big. It was only until I got back to the main menu that I was like surprised about like there's a leaderboard

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:27.780
with like 10 positions on it and they're all named Target Renegade until you like, you know, basically play through the game

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:29.040
and add your high scorer.

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:34.780
That said, like, I don't know, did this feel like an arcade game to you

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:42.440
or did it feel a little more narratively connected than what you expect from the kind of arcade games that have leaderboards?

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:44.580
I mean, I felt zero narrative.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:57.500
Aside from the setup of go rescue your brother and these kind of cookie cutter plates that'll be set up between levels,

00:20:57.560 --> 00:20:59.940
they're just like, good job, kid, you did it.

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:07.020
Now, you know, the park is easy, but now we're going to go uptown.

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:10.640
And I didn't really feel any kind of narrative.

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:12.720
Yeah, I guess that wasn't the right wording.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:17.200
I guess what I'm thinking is, is like now that I've played a few more of these on the NES,

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:24.120
I just was surprised to think of like a leaderboard and like score being the kind of thing you go for.

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:25.520
Yeah, that is definitely weird.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:28.380
It's not even just after you finish.

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:31.440
It's the first thing you see is this leaderboard.

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:38.700
And I guess like their assumption was a bunch of friends were going to boot the game up

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:42.420
and just leave it on for hours while they each attempt it.

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:43.720
Yeah, there's no saving. Yeah.

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:49.200
Yeah, because otherwise it's going to reset to the same score for everyone.

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:53.500
That's why it says today's leaderboard or today's highest scores.

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:55.000
It's a weird addition.

00:21:56.560 --> 00:22:08.600
But I also think maybe what you were saying about story mode as a story mode in comparison to our other games

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:14.100
is because the other games that we've played have been a bit more open recently, like like River City.

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:17.200
Like that was like an RPG beat-em-up.

00:22:17.420 --> 00:22:21.860
And it's actually kind of been a while since we've played one that's just like level one, two, three.

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:34.680
But Sean, you did mention the music and the guitar solos and everything like that.

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:40.980
So I feel like it's obligatory for me to mention that Tim Fallon did the music for this game.

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:42.180
And you don't have to know who that is.

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:43.720
I never really knew who that was.

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:49.440
I don't. Except for the fact that he, you know, we'll probably talk the whole episode about it.

00:22:49.660 --> 00:22:53.260
He did the music for Pictionary on the NES.

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:55.040
Pictionary. Yes.

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:56.320
And I feel bad.

00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:57.880
I don't really want to spoil it for anybody.

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:01.460
But basically, Pictionary on the NES.

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:04.140
He did Silver Surfer on the NES.

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:07.100
He did a bunch of Super Nintendo games.

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:13.280
Well, he's still, I think like up until 2020, still making like every now and again like a track or whatever for something.

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:18.540
But basically, like this guy dominated on the 8 and 16 bit hardware.

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:21.860
Like just for that kind of stuff.

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:30.540
Like the most intense tracks that don't even necessarily like, I think he even said in an interview that he doesn't get to play the games or anything like that.

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:35.380
He just gets to imagine what might happen and write music based off that.

00:23:35.380 --> 00:23:36.860
And then they just throw it into the game.

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:42.140
You'll definitely know that that's true when we get to Pictionary, if you know what I mean.

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:45.880
Okay. I will just have to hold my breath for that.

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:57.860
Yeah. And then on the sequels and spinoff side of everything, we talked about the various versions of Target Renegade where either your brothers murdered or kidnapped.

00:23:58.340 --> 00:24:04.340
I wonder what the conclusive thing then, though, is in Renegade 3, the final chapter.

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:06.600
Like did they take a stand on which one was canon?

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:17.640
Because there is a third Renegade, but unlike this one is like a time traveling one and you're rescuing your captured girlfriend.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:23.960
So I guess like they don't they don't care what happened to your brother because now it's your girlfriend.

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:30.000
Well, maybe your second player, if this is actually a multiplayer game this time, is your brother.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:38.040
Well, they actually dropped the two player mode in Renegade 3, which feels like the biggest misstep for a sequel.

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:40.060
Well, I mean, this one did the same thing.

00:24:40.460 --> 00:24:42.840
Yeah, but I guess I'm saying like, you know, you want more features.

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:49.500
And this game also didn't come to the NES, so we don't have to play Renegade 3, which is just great.

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:56.360
Oh, good. Not to color, of course, our thoughts about the upcoming segment.

00:24:56.360 --> 00:25:01.080
But before we get to the upcoming segment, I thought I was mentioning that nobody says this about the NES version,

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:12.040
but the ZX Spectrum version of Target Renegade was voted number 13 in the top 100 ZX Spectrum games of all time,

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:16.720
according to the like Sinclair Spectrum fanzine.

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:23.680
So respected people who like ZX Spectrum specifically voted on this.

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:25.260
So that's like, it's already niche.

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:26.640
It's a niche console.

00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:29.760
Then it's a niche kind of game for release on that console.

00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:32.460
And then it gets number 13 out of 100.

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:36.760
So either the ZX Spectrum has a pretty weak library,

00:25:37.940 --> 00:25:43.960
or maybe Target Renegade is just a completely different kind of game on the ZX Spectrum.

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:46.040
You don't know the answer to that.

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:47.480
I don't know the answer to that.

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:51.820
And I don't know your answer to our next segment in the Essential Games list.

00:25:54.840 --> 00:26:02.120
Even if Target Renegade is a step forward from Renegade on any particular scale,

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:08.740
whether you want to say about the gameplay, the graphics, the boss battles, whatever it be,

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:13.820
it's actually a step down from the kind of beat-em-ups that we've had recently.

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:18.500
And so for me, almost within like 10 minutes of playing this game,

00:26:18.700 --> 00:26:21.700
I was certain of my vote just based on what I had experienced.

00:26:21.700 --> 00:26:26.180
That's not a good feeling because I feel like at the beginning of a game,

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:30.360
you could actually, that's like, that's your only chance to hook people.

00:26:30.460 --> 00:26:35.400
If you could hook them in early, they might play your mediocre game for longer.

00:26:35.400 --> 00:26:40.020
Like as it continues to like not introduce new concepts and mostly be the same game,

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:41.580
like they were invested at the beginning.

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:43.180
So they'll just kind of slog through it.

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:48.680
Whereas this game, not that it was a slog or boring or anything,

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:56.420
but it didn't do anything revolutionary to the Double Dragons and River City Ransoms of the NES hardware.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:27:02.740
And so I just kind of felt like already I was playing like 25% of a game that I already played.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:05.780
So obviously it can't be on the Essential Games list for me.

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:11.820
But what about you, Sean? Yeah, I didn't have as, and I'm not saying that your reaction is harsh,

00:27:12.020 --> 00:27:14.680
but I'd say it's pretty level.

00:27:15.620 --> 00:27:18.460
But I'd say that I didn't have as harsh of a reaction.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:20.100
I did like the game.

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:21.920
I did enjoy my time with it.

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:28.120
I mostly laughed at all the goofy enemies and the over-the-top music

00:27:28.120 --> 00:27:34.220
and how my HUD had an attitude.

00:27:34.220 --> 00:27:38.380
It had some tood, had some in-your-face action.

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:41.200
So I thought it was a charming game.

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:42.100
But you're right.

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:45.320
Like there isn't much iteration going on here.

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:48.040
It's kind of what we've already played.

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:53.260
Maybe slick in some ways, but underdeveloped in others.

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:54.920
So I agree.

00:27:54.920 --> 00:27:56.780
It's not on the Essential Games list.

00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:58.160
But I did enjoy it.

00:27:58.620 --> 00:28:03.340
I do feel like I gave that status bar the short stick here on the episode

00:28:03.340 --> 00:28:05.280
because it didn't really even give it its own section.

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:06.580
Not that we needed to.

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:07.520
I didn't need a section.

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:10.600
Yeah, not that it needed it or that we need to do it now.

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:14.920
But it is kind of also, in a weird way,

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:19.440
it's something you notice from time to time.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:21.720
You don't always, like when that text pops up,

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:23.960
I don't always feel obligated to read it.

00:28:24.260 --> 00:28:27.260
I occasionally glance down there if that made sense.

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:29.040
Yeah, exactly. Because mostly it's just saying,

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:32.900
I wasn't like, oh, what does it have to say? Go right, go left, watch your back.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:35.100
But then it's like, yo, dude.

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:39.400
Yeah, like it should have had personality 100% of the time.

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:44.380
Yeah. Okay, but I think this podcast has personality 100% of the time.

00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:47.000
What about you? Like 40% to 50%.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:48.140
Oh, okay, yeah.

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:49.920
Well, 100% now without Joe.

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:52.880
No, no, no.

00:28:53.280 --> 00:28:56.760
That's just a cheap shot while he's not here to defend himself.

00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:00.360
Joe actually makes up 33% of the podcast,

00:29:00.360 --> 00:29:01.300
if you can believe it.
